00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.740
as the Wikipedia says to the
Google Cloud Platform Podcast

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.290
to celebrate episode 100.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.319
Vint, thank you so much
for joining us today.

00:01:23.319 --> 00:01:24.610
VINT CERF: I am just delighted.

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:26.290
This is the kind
of conversation I

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:29.110
live for so I am looking
forward to the questions

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.299
and the repartee.

00:01:30.299 --> 00:01:31.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, I'm incredibly

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.200
excited because I've been
a huge fan for a very

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.690
long time, obviously.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:39.310
But also because I took a
selfie with you three years ago

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.530
in a cafe in Mountain View.

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:43.050
And since then,
I've always wanted

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:44.300
to have a longer conversation.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.410
So all of this podcast
was just as an excuse

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.076
to get to meet you again,
so thank you for joining us.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:50.575
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:50.575 --> 00:01:52.930
VINT CERF: Well, as I say,
I really am delighted.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.730
And there is so much
to say about how

00:01:54.730 --> 00:02:00.316
Google has transformed its world
in the Google Cloud Platform.

00:02:00.316 --> 00:02:01.690
It's something
that we can all be

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.990
proud of but also recognize
huge amount of work

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:09.100
lying ahead to make it more
useful for our own internal

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.601
purposes and for the users that
we have all around the world.

00:02:13.601 --> 00:02:14.350
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:16.683
Well, before we get stuck
into all that stuff-- because,

00:02:16.683 --> 00:02:18.100
oh my God, I'm so excited.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.100
If anyone hasn't had the
pleasure of knowing, reading,

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.270
or hearing about Vint Cerf--

00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:23.980
Vint, do you want to give
us a little bit about you

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.990
and your background, and
what you do at Google?

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.420
VINT CERF: Well, sure,
I'd be happy to do that.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:30.646
I've been at Google since 2005.

00:02:30.646 --> 00:02:34.750
I joined as Vice President
and Chief Internet Evangelist.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:38.440
And I confess to you that I
did not ask for that title.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.737
I was asked what title did
I want, and I said Archduke.

00:02:42.737 --> 00:02:43.236
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:43.236 --> 00:02:46.064
And Larry, and Eric, and Sergey
ran off, and they came back

00:02:46.064 --> 00:02:47.980
and they said, you know,
the previous Archduke

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.220
was Ferdinand, and he
was assassinated in 1914,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.710
and it started World
War I. Maybe that's

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:54.900
not a good title to have.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.935
Why don't you be our
Chief Internet Evangelist.

00:02:56.935 --> 00:02:57.942
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:57.942 --> 00:03:00.250
And I said, OK, I can
do that, considering

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:02.980
that I'd been doing
it for about 35 years

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.200
before I joined Google.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:08.320
So of course, before
that I had many roles

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:12.370
to play in the evolution
of the internet.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.330
And I'd like to think I
still have a role to play.

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.180
Eric Schmidt, our
executive chairman,

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.700
pointed out to me that I
wasn't allowed to retire.

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:22.780
I said, why not?

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.570
And he said, because you're
only half-done, there's still

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.805
3 and 1/2 billion
people to convert,

00:03:27.805 --> 00:03:29.540
to get up on the internet.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.220
And he's right about that.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.892
There's still much
work to be done.

00:03:32.892 --> 00:03:35.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool, so that
actually ties up really well

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.930
with one of our first
questions, which is so what

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.440
does Vint Cerf do day to day?

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.820
VINT CERF: Well, fortunately
my life is not repetitive.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.130
Every day is new and different.

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.690
I have many meetings.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.330
Like today, for example, I
was at the Inter-American

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.210
Development Bank for
the morning meeting

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:58.210
with people who are interested
in making major investments

00:03:58.210 --> 00:03:59.090
in infrastructure.

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:01.510
They're trying to
understand how they

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.700
can take actions that
will improve access

00:04:03.700 --> 00:04:06.520
to the internet in
the developing world.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.430
And they're trying to
understand what conditions

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:13.330
need to be in place so that
any investment they might make

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.310
will have productive results.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.350
And in that case,
of course we're

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.779
worried about the
entire ecosystem

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:23.800
surrounding the implementation
and operation of the internet

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:26.410
and the creation
of new applications

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.650
that will be useful to people
locally in local languages,

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:32.320
in addition to
facilitating visibility

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.620
for companies in the rest of
the world, which is something

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.980
we try to do for people who
are advertising their products

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:40.130
and services.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:43.060
So I spend a lot of time,
both here in Washington

00:04:43.060 --> 00:04:46.840
and on the road, on
policy-related matters that

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.110
have to do with the use
and abuse of the internet.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:51.640
That's another big
theme, which is

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.460
that once you put the general
public up on the internet,

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.750
you get what you
sort of deserve,

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.780
which is the general
public, including

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.320
people that don't have your
best interests in mind.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.300
And so a big issue
for us and others

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.100
that offer services
on the net is

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:09.700
how to deal with people
who inject bad information

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.640
onto the net or who
commit fraud, who bully

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.300
people, who distribute malware.

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.280
There are a variety
of things that we

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.540
are well aware of
thanks to headlines

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.850
that are harmful in the
network environment.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:27.710
And so our job is
to do everything

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:31.130
we can at Google to limit
the kinds of damage that

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.460
can be done while at the same
time enhancing and enabling

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:39.620
all kinds of very positive
applications on the network.

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:44.450
So our search engine
is very much a tool

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.430
that people use
to find out what's

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.980
available on the network,
what products and services are

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:53.120
there, not just on the net,
but in the general nearby areas

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.020
or even around the world?

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.710
So part of my job
is to help people

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:01.430
think their way through what
conditions they need to create,

00:06:01.430 --> 00:06:05.690
if they don't already exist,
to both build and operate

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:09.470
pieces of the internet and then
construct applications on top--

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:12.590
how to enable it to be
beneficial to the people who

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:17.120
are trying to use this
far-flung global system.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:19.790
I'm in the research
group it turns out,

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.710
and report up to
John Giannandrea,

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:24.740
so I also get exposed to
a lot of the things that

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:26.820
are going on on
the research side,

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.940
especially in artificial
intelligence, machine

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.620
learning, quantum
computing, and things

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:34.790
like that, which are well
beyond my personal capability,

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:36.980
but I'm fascinated by.

00:06:36.980 --> 00:06:41.520
On top of which, Google has
been very generous with my time,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.600
and so I do outside activities
that contribute also to this.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.660
So I sit on the National Science
Board overseeing the National

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:52.270
Science Foundation, which is
doing a great deal of research

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.650
in all the various sciences,
including computer science.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.790
I had been chairman of the
Americas Registry for Internet

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:02.660
Numbers, until the beginning
of this year, which

00:07:02.660 --> 00:07:05.660
oversees the allocation
of internet address space

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:08.771
to people in the Caribbean
and North America.

00:07:08.771 --> 00:07:10.520
I was chairman of the
Internet Corporation

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:14.450
for Assigned Names and
Numbers from 2000 to 2007,

00:07:14.450 --> 00:07:17.420
again a major component of
the internet infrastructure

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.870
managing the domain name
system and top-level IP address

00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:22.220
allocation.

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:24.740
Google has been very
willing to allow

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:28.080
me to spend time in the internet
space in one way or another,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.280
whether it's directly
associated with Google or more

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:33.840
generally with the
internet infrastructure.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.466
And so I'm very
grateful for that.

00:07:35.466 --> 00:07:36.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Fantastic.

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.673
MARK MANDEL: That's so
much stuff to unpack there.

00:07:38.673 --> 00:07:40.757
I think you clearly do
so much amazing things.

00:07:40.757 --> 00:07:42.340
Why don't we sort
of go back a little.

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:45.087
I'm very curious about the
history of some of this stuff.

00:07:45.087 --> 00:07:46.420
It's actually kind of hilarious.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.878
We were talking
about this earlier.

00:07:47.878 --> 00:07:49.277
Francesc is in Paris, France.

00:07:49.277 --> 00:07:50.860
I'm currently in
Melbourne, Australia.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:52.120
You're in Washington.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:54.440
The very network
and infrastructure

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.600
that you fathered, for
lack of a better term,

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:58.730
makes this whole thing possible.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:02.360
But you are known as one of
the fathers of the internet.

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:06.065
So thank you very much for that,
making our jobs all possible.

00:08:06.065 --> 00:08:08.610
VINT CERF: Well, to the extent
that this has been beneficial,

00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:09.830
I think for the credit.

00:08:09.830 --> 00:08:12.260
To the extent that some
damage has been done,

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.884
I refuse to accept
the responsibility.

00:08:13.884 --> 00:08:15.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Thank
you for the good parts.

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:16.350
MARK MANDEL: That
seems fair enough.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:17.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY: There you go.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:17.740
VINT CERF: Thank you very much.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:18.860
MARK MANDEL: Thank you
for all the good parts.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:20.300
But I'm super curious.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:23.540
I mean, when you were working in
this, back at the origin state.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:26.020
Many people consider
the internet sort of--

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.770
for those of us who
have become used to it

00:08:27.770 --> 00:08:29.978
and who have access to it
become almost a human right

00:08:29.978 --> 00:08:31.670
or a requisite
part of existence.

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:34.757
Did you ever see that in the
pathway of this distributed

00:08:34.757 --> 00:08:35.840
network you were building?

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:38.630
VINT CERF: Well, I certainly
never anticipated the notion

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.870
that access to internet might
be considered a human right.

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:45.860
And as you may know, I have
published at least one op-ed

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:49.010
that suggested that that
was not a good formulation.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:50.960
I'd like to come back to that.

00:08:50.960 --> 00:08:55.070
But let's go back to the
origins of this system.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:58.640
It was originally funded by the
American Defense Department.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.270
My partner in the design is
Robert Kahn, who at the time

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:05.420
was at the Defense Advanced
Research Projects Agency.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.670
This is way back in
late '72 or early

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.665
'73 of the previous century.

00:09:09.665 --> 00:09:12.830
God, that sounds
like a long time ago.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:13.910
And it was, actually.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:17.720
So Bob had this idea that open
networking would be a good

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:22.670
thing, that the packet switching
technology of the ARPANET

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:25.610
project could be replicated
in multiple networks--

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:29.050
some that are mobile
radio, some over satellite,

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:31.140
some over a dedicated wireline--

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.860
and that these
multiplicity packets,

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:37.640
which networks needed to
be interconnected in a way

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.620
that created commonality.

00:09:39.620 --> 00:09:41.480
And the only way we
could do that was

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.670
to develop a set
of protocols that

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:45.650
would let these disparate
packets, which nets

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:49.970
look like one gigantic network,
although made up of many nets.

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.090
So the internet is a
network of networks.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:58.210
We developed the protocols and
the concepts to do that in 1973

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:03.260
and published a paper in 1974,
developed the detailed specs

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:08.120
for the protocols in late '74,
started implementation in 1975,

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:11.360
ran through a series of
iterations, four of them or so,

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.990
stabilized that design in
1978, started implementing it

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:16.970
on many different
operating systems,

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:21.240
turned the system on
on January 1, 1983.

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:23.690
Now, you might say did you
have any idea that it was

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.310
going to be global in scope?

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:28.000
The answer is absolutely,
because we imagined

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.980
this would be needed by the
American Defense Department

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:32.520
for command and control.

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:35.480
That means it has to work
no matter where the Defense

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:38.270
Department deployed, which
meant it had to work everywhere

00:10:38.270 --> 00:10:39.420
in the world.

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:43.070
So we were already thinking
in global terms at the time

00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:45.000
that this design was being done.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:46.700
We actually didn't
quite get it right.

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:51.830
We thought that a 32-bit
internet numerical address

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:55.140
space would be enough at
least to do an experiment.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:59.384
So we designed the system to
have 4.3 billion terminations.

00:10:59.384 --> 00:11:00.800
And that, I think,
might have been

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.110
more than there were people
in the world at the time we

00:11:03.110 --> 00:11:04.650
were doing this work.

00:11:04.650 --> 00:11:08.150
However, we ran out around 2011.

00:11:08.150 --> 00:11:11.420
Fortunately the engineers
who do the standards

00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:14.480
for the internet in the
Internet Engineering Task Force

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.580
could foresee that we
were going to run out

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:20.420
and designed a new format
for the internet packets that

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:23.470
had a 128-bit addressing space.

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:25.220
Now, we're not talking
about domain names

00:11:25.220 --> 00:11:28.180
which are alphanumeric--
for example, Google.com.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:32.130
We're talking about an
underlying numerical address

00:11:32.130 --> 00:11:34.640
space sort of like
phone numbers,

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:37.160
although the analogy
sometimes doesn't work.

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:39.060
But it's similar in nature.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:42.980
So 128-bit address
space is also referred

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.710
to as IP version 6,
internet protocol version

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:50.210
6, Which gives you 3.4 times
10 to the 38 addresses, which

00:11:50.210 --> 00:11:52.700
is a big number.

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:56.540
Google has been at the
forefront of implementing IPv6

00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:59.600
for the various applications
that it supports,

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:03.890
and it will continue to
support both v4 and v6 address

00:12:03.890 --> 00:12:08.720
spaces until such time as v4
seems to be no longer needed.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:11.480
So we are among the
good guys who have been

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.700
pushing v6 implementations.

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:17.559
It's probably got to about
30% worldwide on average,

00:12:17.559 --> 00:12:19.100
although there are
some networks that

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:22.310
are 100% capable of doing
both the older version

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:25.071
32-bit addresses
and the 128-bit.

00:12:25.071 --> 00:12:27.320
And there are some networks
that haven't done anything

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:28.190
yet at all.

00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:30.660
So there's still
work to be done.

00:12:30.660 --> 00:12:35.330
The internet of things, which
is threatening to overwhelm us

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:38.150
in terms of numbers
of devices on the net,

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:41.930
could have as many as 50
billion devices in the system

00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:45.560
by the early 2020s,
which we'll definitely

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.230
need the IPv6 expanded
address space.

00:12:48.230 --> 00:12:51.900
Mobile phones also
are dependent on this.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:57.020
The thing called LTE or
long term evolution, which

00:12:57.020 --> 00:13:00.650
is sometimes called
4G, also makes

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.060
use of IPv6 address space.

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:05.390
So you can see this
proliferation of devices

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:07.040
is driving that.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.130
Finally, with regard to
internet of things, something

00:13:10.130 --> 00:13:11.840
our company cares
a great deal about,

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:15.020
there are both the
production of these devices,

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:17.190
at Nest, for example,
and at Google,

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:20.210
and there is the cloud
component of this,

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:22.340
which is helping to
monitor the devices,

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:24.800
to provide artificial
intelligence

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:27.190
applications through those
devices, such as Google

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:31.400
Assistant by way of Google
Home, or by way of the Pixel,

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.120
or the Chromebooks, or
other kinds of equipment

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:36.560
that Google makes.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:40.820
So we are now faced with
a proliferation of devices

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:43.400
and also a great deal
of concern for safety,

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.460
and security, privacy,
and reliability,

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:50.720
of this increasingly
large system of devices

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:52.700
all interacting with each other.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.840
And so that's certainly
going to be a challenge,

00:13:54.840 --> 00:13:57.770
not only for Google but for
all the other parties who

00:13:57.770 --> 00:14:00.934
are beginning to participate
in the internet of things.

00:14:00.934 --> 00:14:02.600
Now, there's lots
more to be said there,

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:04.490
but let me stop
there and just say

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:09.470
that there's lots that I've
been finding myself engaged in

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:13.070
because of this huge investment
that we and others are making

00:14:13.070 --> 00:14:15.210
in internet-enabled devices.

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:19.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So to go back
a little bit to the more society

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:22.220
side of the internet,
I'm curious about

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:26.203
were you expecting this
huge change in society?

00:14:26.203 --> 00:14:30.090
Literally, the internet has
changed the way we do things.

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.990
We communicate, we
work, we do everything.

00:14:32.990 --> 00:14:34.910
Was that something
you expected too?

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:35.910
VINT CERF: Yes.

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:42.260
And I can prove this in the
following sense-- in 1992,

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.540
I became the founding
president of something

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.360
called the Internet Society.

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:48.110
And why did we call it that?

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:51.710
Because I believe that a
society would emerge out

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:53.210
of the internet.

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:54.950
And indeed it has.

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:58.910
That society may not be
one that we like 100%.

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.680
And this is what
happens when you

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:03.830
create new infrastructure--
people find ways to use it

00:15:03.830 --> 00:15:05.255
and to abuse it.

00:15:05.255 --> 00:15:07.820
This is like cars and the
roads that they drive on.

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:09.590
People get drunk, and
they drive the cars

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:12.080
and run into each other
and run into things,

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.810
and kill themselves
or harm other people.

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.120
The internet has its downsides.

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:23.910
It has lowered the barrier
to access to information.

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:28.860
It has lowered the barrier for
expression to practically zero.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:32.600
And I've always thought that was
a good thing, that we want more

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.300
ability for people to
both express themselves,

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:37.190
and to share what they
know, and to find out

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.860
what other people
are willing to share.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:41.780
But at the same
time, we also have

00:15:41.780 --> 00:15:44.690
to recognize that there are
people who get on the net

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:47.930
and do not have the rest of
the society's best interests

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:49.050
at heart.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:50.960
So we worry about
fraud, we worry

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.390
about false information,
or misinformation,

00:15:53.390 --> 00:15:56.640
or so-called fake news
and things like that.

00:15:56.640 --> 00:15:59.900
We worry about malware that
people create and propagate

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:02.570
through the network
and cause harm

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:05.630
to devices that are
attached to the network

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:09.440
or essentially grab
control over those devices

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:12.170
and turn them into what
are called botnets, which

00:16:12.170 --> 00:16:14.600
are sort of robotic
networks, that can then

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:17.300
be used to generate
denial of service attacks

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:20.990
or to generate spam, or to
do other kinds of things that

00:16:20.990 --> 00:16:23.100
are potentially harmful.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:29.210
So this huge global system,
which has literally,

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:31.640
I would say,
millions of networks,

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:35.310
including the local one that
you run at home, for instance,

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:38.300
and then the big gigantic
globe-spanning networks

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:41.030
of the telcos and the cablecos.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:44.990
This system is, like every
other infrastructure,

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:47.000
capable of being abused.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.550
And we're experiencing
the effects

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.440
of this society
which is emerging out

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:53.180
of this technology.

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:55.970
So now we have to
struggle with protecting

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:59.600
people against harmful
experiences online.

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:02.720
And sometimes we can
build technical means

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.950
of protecting them-- detecting
malware, for example,

00:17:06.950 --> 00:17:09.349
defending against denial
of service attacks,

00:17:09.349 --> 00:17:14.030
filtering out spam, which we do
very well in our Gmail service.

00:17:14.030 --> 00:17:19.010
So we have these technical
means of protecting people--

00:17:19.010 --> 00:17:21.079
two-factor authentication
to keep accounts

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:23.839
from being seized or hijacked.

00:17:23.839 --> 00:17:28.940
But you can't sure that you
can prevent all harmful acts

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:32.060
from occurring on the net
through technical means.

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:35.060
And so you also have to build
in mechanisms for saying,

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:38.510
if we detect or catch you
doing some of these bad things,

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:40.830
there will be consequences.

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.370
And here now we get into
international agreements,

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:46.790
because the network
is global in scope.

00:17:46.790 --> 00:17:50.450
And a perpetrator of harm
may be in one jurisdiction,

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:53.870
and their victim
in another across

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:56.150
international boundaries,
which immediately

00:17:56.150 --> 00:17:59.060
says that we need to have
cooperative interactions

00:17:59.060 --> 00:18:00.560
among the countries
that are part

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:02.630
of the internet environment.

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.840
There are just so many issues
here that we have to cope with,

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:11.060
not only as Google but also
more broadly as citizens

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:13.310
in the online environment.

00:18:13.310 --> 00:18:16.130
So I'm actually spending
a lot of my time

00:18:16.130 --> 00:18:19.310
asking questions about policy,
for example, and how it can

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:21.050
be implemented and enforced.

00:18:21.050 --> 00:18:22.670
Where does the policy come from?

00:18:22.670 --> 00:18:26.600
Can we use multistakeholder
processes to establish policy?

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:30.530
And then use law enforcement
and trade agreements

00:18:30.530 --> 00:18:34.580
and other kinds of
things to enforce safety

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.850
for the people who are online
to enforce their privacy,

00:18:37.850 --> 00:18:41.530
to protect them against
various forms of abuse.

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:43.160
And I might point
out one more thing,

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:45.364
as long as I'm
rattling on here--

00:18:45.364 --> 00:18:46.655
MARK MANDEL: [LAUGHS] Go ahead.

00:18:46.655 --> 00:18:51.320
VINT CERF: That this lowering
of barrier for speech means that

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:54.030
you and I and
others on the net--

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:57.620
the 3 and 1/2 billion or so
that we believe are online--

00:18:57.620 --> 00:19:00.770
now have an additional
challenge before them.

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:02.780
And that is to think
critically about what

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:05.390
we're seeing and hearing
in this environment,

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:08.780
because it's easy to
inject malware or easy--

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.810
I should say, inject
false information

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:15.060
or mistaken information
in the system.

00:19:15.060 --> 00:19:18.950
And our search engine finds both
the good stuff and sometimes

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:20.390
the not-so-good stuff.

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:22.370
And so as users
of the network, we

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:24.890
suddenly inherit
this responsibility

00:19:24.890 --> 00:19:26.960
for thinking critically
about what we're actually

00:19:26.960 --> 00:19:29.000
seeing and hearing
and questioning,

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:30.030
where did it come from?

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:32.470
What's the basis for my
belief that this is useful

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:34.317
versus bad information?

00:19:34.317 --> 00:19:36.400
I think a lot of people
are a little uncomfortable

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:39.260
that they suddenly inherited
this responsibility.

00:19:39.260 --> 00:19:42.620
But indeed, if you want to use
the network to best effect,

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.452
you actually have to think
critically about that.

00:19:45.452 --> 00:19:46.910
MARK MANDEL: So I
think this segues

00:19:46.910 --> 00:19:49.160
into an interesting topic,
as well, when we're talking

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:51.920
about the safety of the
internet and malware

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:53.850
and also, from a
societal point of view,

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.090
how do you think this affects
either net neutrality,

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:57.470
or the idea of net neutrality?

00:19:57.470 --> 00:19:59.150
Does it have any
impact on how we

00:19:59.150 --> 00:20:00.450
should be thinking about that?

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:02.500
VINT CERF: So this is
a very good question.

00:20:02.500 --> 00:20:05.990
The term net neutrality, as
I'm sure your listeners know,

00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:09.270
is used in different ways in
different parts of the world.

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:12.380
So we have to be careful
about the use of the term

00:20:12.380 --> 00:20:15.510
and ask, OK, in what context
are we asking the question?

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:17.550
In the United
States, for instance,

00:20:17.550 --> 00:20:21.290
during the early 1990s, there
were about 8,000 internet

00:20:21.290 --> 00:20:22.770
service providers.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:24.450
This was a dial up system.

00:20:24.450 --> 00:20:28.340
And to reach a particular
internet server or service,

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:29.960
you just dialed a
particular number

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.270
that got you to a modem bank.

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:34.750
If you didn't like the
service, it was easy to switch.

00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:36.390
You just dialed a
different number.

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:39.800
And so there was quite a bit of
user choice in terms of access

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:40.820
to the internet.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:43.250
Along comes broadband,
and suddenly there

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:45.620
are very few providers
of broadband service--

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:49.790
the telcos, the cablecos,
and that's about it.

00:20:49.790 --> 00:20:53.270
So although in
recent times, we're

00:20:53.270 --> 00:20:56.120
starting to see wireless
services increasing

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:58.310
in their data rate, so that
they could be considered

00:20:58.310 --> 00:21:00.120
broadband as well.

00:21:00.120 --> 00:21:02.210
So during this period--

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:07.070
roughly in the very late
1990s, early 2000s--

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:10.250
in the US, if you were
looking at broadband service

00:21:10.250 --> 00:21:12.894
as a consumer, you didn't
have very much choice.

00:21:12.894 --> 00:21:14.810
If you were in the rural
parts of the country,

00:21:14.810 --> 00:21:16.790
you had zero choice
for broadband.

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:19.160
No one was providing
that service.

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:21.650
If you were in the
suburban areas,

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:24.710
you might have a choice of
either a telco or a cableco.

00:21:24.710 --> 00:21:27.050
And in the urban areas,
you might have a choice

00:21:27.050 --> 00:21:28.940
of two suppliers of broadband.

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:32.810
So not everyone was uniformly
able to get competitive access

00:21:32.810 --> 00:21:33.900
to broadband service.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.150
This is still a problem today.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:38.780
And the absence of
enough competition

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:40.970
and choice for the consumers--

00:21:40.970 --> 00:21:44.690
there's always the risk that a
monopoly supplier will in fact

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:49.010
interfere with people's freedom
to use the network any way

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:49.560
they want.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.890
For example, if you in
the vertical business

00:21:51.890 --> 00:21:55.670
of providing video services in
addition to underlying internet

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:58.790
service, it might be
tempting to interfere

00:21:58.790 --> 00:22:01.760
with a competitor's
video service going

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.620
through the internet channel.

00:22:03.620 --> 00:22:06.440
And in fact, there
were some indications

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:08.720
of this kind of bad
behavior on the part of some

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:12.260
of the broadband carriers who
would inhibit voiceover IP

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.200
or would interfere
with streaming video

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:19.190
in order to induce customers
to use their service,

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:23.690
or possibly to interfere with
intermediate carriers who

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:27.260
were trying to carry traffic
and who were getting squeezed.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:29.720
And therefore quality
of service suffered.

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:35.080
So we have what I think are
fewer choices as consumers.

00:22:35.080 --> 00:22:39.050
And as the choice goes down,
then the risk factors go up.

00:22:39.050 --> 00:22:40.970
And so now you need
some regulation

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:43.280
in order to assure that
the consumer interests are

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:44.210
protected.

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:46.880
So the net neutrality
rules mostly, as I see it,

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:50.840
in the US are intended
to limit the ability

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:54.230
of a broadband internet
service access provider

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:57.050
to interfere with what
you do with the network,

00:22:57.050 --> 00:22:59.480
where you go on the net,
what products and services

00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:01.160
you exercise.

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:04.370
That's why it's been a
big issue here in the US.

00:23:04.370 --> 00:23:08.390
The ability of the Federal
Communications Commission

00:23:08.390 --> 00:23:16.370
to step in and defend the users'
choices was limited for a time

00:23:16.370 --> 00:23:20.150
because there was a
choice made by the FCC

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:23.120
to treat the internet as
an unregulated information

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:23.810
service.

00:23:23.810 --> 00:23:26.060
And subsequently
there was an attempt

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:30.770
to put in principles for
net neutrality or defense

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:34.310
against the practices that
were anti-competitive.

00:23:34.310 --> 00:23:39.090
When the FCC tried to
enforce those principles,

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:41.300
it was told by the
Supreme Court that you

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:44.870
didn't have a legal basis
for exercising that control,

00:23:44.870 --> 00:23:48.200
because the FCC had decided
that the internet was

00:23:48.200 --> 00:23:49.460
an unregulated service.

00:23:49.460 --> 00:23:51.495
So they had no basis
for regulation.

00:23:51.495 --> 00:23:56.540
This caused the FCC,
under Tom Wheeler,

00:23:56.540 --> 00:24:00.730
to shift the internet
service into Title II, which

00:24:00.730 --> 00:24:04.790
is the telecom title,
re-instantiating the FCC's

00:24:04.790 --> 00:24:06.650
ability to regulate.

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:09.050
And now, of course, under
the new administration,

00:24:09.050 --> 00:24:11.570
Ajit Pai, the
current chairman, is

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:14.630
trying to undo that
particular decision

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:18.680
and to put the internet back
into an unregulated state,

00:24:18.680 --> 00:24:21.980
which means that there would be
no legal basis for protecting

00:24:21.980 --> 00:24:25.310
users whose freedom of
choice may have been harmed

00:24:25.310 --> 00:24:28.760
by a non-neutral provider.

00:24:28.760 --> 00:24:30.470
So at this stage
in the US, there's

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:33.770
some uncertainty about
protecting user interests.

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:36.440
In other parts of the world,
there may be enough competition

00:24:36.440 --> 00:24:39.590
so the users can simply
choose a different carrier

00:24:39.590 --> 00:24:41.780
or different internet
service provider

00:24:41.780 --> 00:24:45.200
in order to get service in there
and thereby use competition

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.940
as a balancing effect.

00:24:46.940 --> 00:24:49.610
Here in the US, there
isn't enough competition,

00:24:49.610 --> 00:24:53.009
and so regulation seems to
be the only alternative.

00:24:53.009 --> 00:24:54.800
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is really concerning.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:56.760
I was actually unaware
of the last part,

00:24:56.760 --> 00:24:58.160
so thank you for sharing that.

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:00.230
Since you're already
mentioning access

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:03.380
to the internet on other
parts of the world,

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:05.450
I'm curious about,
since you were

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:08.360
able to predict a little
bit that the internet would

00:25:08.360 --> 00:25:11.180
be a global thing, and
it changed the world,

00:25:11.180 --> 00:25:14.120
do you think that having
the rest of the world

00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:16.731
get access to the internet
will change it again?

00:25:16.731 --> 00:25:18.480
Or it will be more of
a progressive thing?

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:20.355
VINT CERF: Well, it will
certainly change it.

00:25:20.355 --> 00:25:22.230
For one thing, half
the people in the world

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:23.490
don't have access yet.

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:25.650
And we already know
some of the things that

00:25:25.650 --> 00:25:28.320
have happened, positive and
negative, for the people who

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:29.906
are already online.

00:25:29.906 --> 00:25:31.530
The reason that I
think it will change,

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:33.540
although maybe not
in predictable ways,

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:36.330
is that the people who
will get access next,

00:25:36.330 --> 00:25:38.730
the next billion
or two, probably

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:41.160
will get access through a
wireless method rather than

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.360
a wired method.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:45.630
They probably will have
smart mobile phones

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:49.590
which now are heavily
populated all around the world.

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:54.880
They will have needs which
have evolved over time.

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:58.410
And so those of us who have
been online, especially

00:25:58.410 --> 00:26:01.200
in the broadband world, let's
say, for the last 15 years

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:04.290
or so have experienced
the internet largely

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:05.702
through laptops.

00:26:05.702 --> 00:26:07.410
And now, of course,
the rest of the world

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:10.200
is going to experience the
internet through smartphones

00:26:10.200 --> 00:26:13.500
and possibly other smart devices
as the internet of things

00:26:13.500 --> 00:26:15.090
continues to deploy.

00:26:15.090 --> 00:26:19.590
So their experiences and
their needs for service

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:21.570
may turn out to be quite
different from what we

00:26:21.570 --> 00:26:23.700
experienced 10 or 15 years ago.

00:26:23.700 --> 00:26:26.550
The consequence of that is
that there is opportunity

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.080
everywhere.

00:26:28.080 --> 00:26:31.260
And on top of which,
solutions to issues

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:35.040
arising in this vastly
increased demand

00:26:35.040 --> 00:26:36.870
may actually come
from the people

00:26:36.870 --> 00:26:39.180
who are new to the
internet as opposed

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:41.310
to people who've been
around for a while.

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:43.860
Don't forget that
intelligence is uniformly

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:45.900
distributed around the world.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:48.300
But a lot of the
smart people may not

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:52.410
be able to exercise their
intelligence to good effect

00:26:52.410 --> 00:26:54.840
with regard to internet, because
the opportunities may not

00:26:54.840 --> 00:26:55.830
be there.

00:26:55.830 --> 00:26:59.950
There may not be incentive
for investment, for example.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:02.820
They may not have the moral
equivalent of venture capital

00:27:02.820 --> 00:27:07.610
and risk taking, which is
endemic in the Silicon Valley,

00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:10.620
less so in other
parts of the world.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:14.220
So I think that
the newer inductees

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:18.510
into the internet family
will experience new needs

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:22.320
and come up with new
requirements, but also

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:25.920
new solutions, which
may and propagate back

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:28.050
into the rest of the internet.

00:27:28.050 --> 00:27:32.250
So to give you an example,
in China I've been told,

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:33.740
in the eastern
part of China, that

00:27:33.740 --> 00:27:35.280
don't bother carrying cash.

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:38.130
Everything is paid
for with the mobile.

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:41.460
And even the beggars hold
up their little mobiles

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:47.100
and say, take a picture
of this little quad symbol

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:50.700
and transfer money
to me, which that has

00:27:50.700 --> 00:27:53.220
to be a very weird feeling--

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:55.839
somebody who's begging using
a smartphone, and saying,

00:27:55.839 --> 00:27:56.630
hey, wait a minute.

00:27:56.630 --> 00:27:58.480
You have a smartphone,
and you're begging?

00:27:58.480 --> 00:27:59.940
How does that work?

00:27:59.940 --> 00:28:02.410
But I will say, in
India, for example,

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:04.170
where digital identities
have been issued

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:05.950
to some 800 million people.

00:28:05.950 --> 00:28:08.380
These people suddenly become
visible to the government,

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:10.070
whereas before they were not.

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.940
And they become
eligible for benefits

00:28:11.940 --> 00:28:13.590
that they couldn't get before.

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:15.950
Estonia only has a
million and a half people.

00:28:15.950 --> 00:28:20.460
They are as heavily networked
and internet-enabled

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:22.560
as any country in the world.

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:25.530
And they are an
interesting place to look,

00:28:25.530 --> 00:28:31.470
because when 100 percent of
the population is online with

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:33.540
digital identities
and everything else,

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:37.320
there are things you can do
relying on the fact that 100%

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:40.590
of the people have access,
as opposed to a country like

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:43.620
the US where maybe
80% have access,

00:28:43.620 --> 00:28:44.930
and the other 20% don't.

00:28:44.930 --> 00:28:48.790
So you have to accommodate
that statistic.

00:28:48.790 --> 00:28:53.430
So here we are with examples
of places that are deeply

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:57.915
embedded in the internet system
and in digital applications.

00:28:57.915 --> 00:29:01.350
It's like explorers going out
into a territory which nobody

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:03.690
has been in and
reporting back to us

00:29:03.690 --> 00:29:05.400
what's out there so
we don't have to make

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:06.942
the same mistakes they do.

00:29:06.942 --> 00:29:08.400
MARK MANDEL: Speaking
of explorers,

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:09.858
thinking possibly
way in the future

00:29:09.858 --> 00:29:11.860
or possibly not depending
on some of the advents

00:29:11.860 --> 00:29:14.220
that we've been seeing
lately around interplanetary

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:17.430
and space travel, when you
built the internet as it was,

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:19.920
were you thinking about possibly
interplanetary networks,

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:22.260
having space stations that
currently orbit the earth

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:24.180
and having them communicate
backwards and forwards?

00:29:24.180 --> 00:29:25.830
Was that something that
you built into the protocol

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:27.150
and design of the system?

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:29.550
Or do you think it's going
to change going forward?

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:31.110
VINT CERF: Actually, I
know it's going to change

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:32.276
because we're already there.

00:29:32.276 --> 00:29:34.300
We're operating an
interplanetary system.

00:29:34.300 --> 00:29:39.000
And the TCP/IP
protocol did not work.

00:29:39.000 --> 00:29:42.180
And we-- when I say we, I'm
speaking of the Jet Propulsion

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:48.090
Laboratory at NASA plus now many
of the other NASA laboratories.

00:29:48.090 --> 00:29:50.760
I started work on an
interplanetary extension

00:29:50.760 --> 00:29:53.880
of the internet in 1998 at JPL.

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:58.080
And we very quickly realized
that the TCP protocols

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:01.350
were designed in a low-latency
context with relatively

00:30:01.350 --> 00:30:02.910
high reliability.

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:04.800
In space, you have
the inverse of that.

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:08.190
You have low reliability, and
you have variable and often

00:30:08.190 --> 00:30:11.550
very long latency as we're
talking about hours to days.

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:15.520
So your ping is not your
friend here in terms of network

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:17.422
management and interaction.

00:30:17.422 --> 00:30:18.880
You don't really
know how long it's

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:21.370
going to take for an
interplanetary packet

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:22.600
to get to its destination.

00:30:22.600 --> 00:30:25.840
It may have to hop through
multiple sites, from Earth

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:28.750
to Mars to Jupiter and wherever.

00:30:28.750 --> 00:30:31.870
So we had to develop a whole
new suite of protocols, which

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:34.600
we called the Bundle
Protocol, which

00:30:34.600 --> 00:30:37.960
is an instance of what is called
delay and disruption-tolerant

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:39.370
networking.

00:30:39.370 --> 00:30:41.440
So in fact we have thought
our way through that.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:45.370
We've now deployed first a
prototype system on Mars,

00:30:45.370 --> 00:30:49.720
and now we've deployed the most
recent and fully standardized

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:52.390
protocols on the
International Space Station.

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:54.820
And we hope those same
standardized protocols

00:30:54.820 --> 00:30:56.320
will be on board
spacecraft which

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:59.980
will be going to Mars and
elsewhere during the 2020s.

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:03.040
So in that particular
case, we had

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:05.920
to accept that the original
design simply was not

00:31:05.920 --> 00:31:10.630
capable of accommodating the
kinds of lossy, long, highly

00:31:10.630 --> 00:31:16.330
variable delay that the space
environment presents us with.

00:31:16.330 --> 00:31:19.130
And so that's why we
designed a new system.

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:22.070
But it is interoperable with
the rest of the internet.

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:24.730
It's just that the
rest of the internet

00:31:24.730 --> 00:31:27.850
isn't organized around
that suite of protocols.

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:30.600
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So that is amazing.

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:32.440
Sincerely, I'm really
impressed with that.

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:34.810
I'm wondering now
is there something

00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:39.250
else other than TCP/IP,
which clearly there is.

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:44.140
Are these protocols still based
on the end-to-end principle?

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:45.640
Could you tell us
a little bit, what

00:31:45.640 --> 00:31:46.930
is the end-to-end principle?

00:31:46.930 --> 00:31:50.020
And is that something
that you're forgetting now

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:51.000
to go interplanetary?

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:53.759
Or is it actually
even more important?

00:31:53.759 --> 00:31:55.300
VINT CERF: Well,
end-to-end basically

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:57.190
said you put something
into the network,

00:31:57.190 --> 00:31:59.800
and in theory, it should pop
out looking exactly the same

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:00.530
on the other end.

00:32:00.530 --> 00:32:02.890
And you rely on the fact
the packets aren't being

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:05.020
altered or modified in between.

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:08.800
This is still very much a
principle that lots of us

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:12.340
would like to hang onto as part
of the internet characteristic.

00:32:12.340 --> 00:32:14.920
The reason that's so
valuable is that if you're

00:32:14.920 --> 00:32:20.140
building protocols that are
at the edges of the network,

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:24.010
building up from the
basic internet protocol,

00:32:24.010 --> 00:32:27.010
this end-to-end
principle helps a lot,

00:32:27.010 --> 00:32:29.680
because you can
assume that what was

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.950
put in by some party in some
distant part of the network

00:32:32.950 --> 00:32:35.870
arrives intact at the other end.

00:32:35.870 --> 00:32:37.510
Now there are ways
of assuring that

00:32:37.510 --> 00:32:39.345
by using cryptography,
for example,

00:32:39.345 --> 00:32:41.380
or digital signatures
and other things

00:32:41.380 --> 00:32:45.430
to assure integrity of
the end-to-end concepts.

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:47.230
There have been
some, let me say,

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:49.090
breaks in this
end-to-end notion.

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:52.270
For example, as the internet
deployed commercially

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.360
in the late '80s
and early '90s, we

00:32:55.360 --> 00:32:58.570
saw corporations
building firewalls

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:01.960
around their corporate
assets in order

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:05.260
to try to protect
against attacks coming

00:33:05.260 --> 00:33:08.320
through the network to
their targeted corporate

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:09.440
infrastructure.

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:11.700
And that actually
introduced, down

00:33:11.700 --> 00:33:16.300
in the guts of the network,
something which potentially

00:33:16.300 --> 00:33:19.030
interfered with the
end-to-end principle,

00:33:19.030 --> 00:33:21.390
because in fact the packets
were getting examined,

00:33:21.390 --> 00:33:24.640
they might be destroyed, they
might be perhaps not altered,

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:25.390
necessarily.

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:27.040
Although there is
the possibility

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:29.950
of doing what's called network
address translation, which

00:33:29.950 --> 00:33:33.490
in fact does interfere with
the end-to-end principle,

00:33:33.490 --> 00:33:36.220
because what may
be sent on one end

00:33:36.220 --> 00:33:39.020
is not how it looks when it's
received on the other end.

00:33:39.020 --> 00:33:42.160
So I've never been a big fan
of this whole network address

00:33:42.160 --> 00:33:45.800
translation because it violates
the end-to-end principle.

00:33:45.800 --> 00:33:48.580
I think that over time
we will ultimately

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:53.170
discover that firewall motions
are inadequate to protect

00:33:53.170 --> 00:33:55.420
against hazards on the network.

00:33:55.420 --> 00:33:57.700
And then ultimately every
device on the network

00:33:57.700 --> 00:34:00.130
has to be able to defend itself.

00:34:00.130 --> 00:34:01.750
When the original
design was done,

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:04.690
we assumed that anyone
could talk to anyone else

00:34:04.690 --> 00:34:08.260
because we didn't know who would
usefully be able to do that.

00:34:08.260 --> 00:34:11.139
But we also said you don't
have to talk to anybody if you

00:34:11.139 --> 00:34:12.639
don't want to.

00:34:12.639 --> 00:34:16.239
And so if you receive a packet
from some random source,

00:34:16.239 --> 00:34:18.880
and you can't identify
them, or you haven't gotten

00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:21.230
the handshake that says,
yes, I know who you are,

00:34:21.230 --> 00:34:24.159
and yes, we have cryptographic
shared variables, if you're not

00:34:24.159 --> 00:34:27.010
satisfied, you can throw
the packet away or send back

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:28.510
a rejection notice.

00:34:28.510 --> 00:34:31.159
I expect devices to do that.

00:34:31.159 --> 00:34:33.460
I think that a
responsible design

00:34:33.460 --> 00:34:37.900
is to inhibit the device from
doing anything which it doesn't

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:41.080
think it is safe, which means
don't talk to people that you

00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:42.699
can't identify.

00:34:42.699 --> 00:34:45.580
So I think that
we are moving back

00:34:45.580 --> 00:34:50.050
towards the end-to-end principle
and with the philosophy

00:34:50.050 --> 00:34:53.290
that you don't have to
communicate with some device

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:55.360
that you don't recognize.

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:57.930
And in fact, you
should enforce that.

00:34:57.930 --> 00:35:00.490
And I think that will actually
improve some of the safety

00:35:00.490 --> 00:35:01.827
and security on the network.

00:35:01.827 --> 00:35:02.785
MARK MANDEL: Fantastic.

00:35:02.785 --> 00:35:05.300
I am just keeping a very
close eye on the time.

00:35:05.300 --> 00:35:06.670
So we've been talking
about very serious stuff,

00:35:06.670 --> 00:35:08.440
and I want to make sure we
throw in some more fun things

00:35:08.440 --> 00:35:09.660
in here, as well.

00:35:09.660 --> 00:35:12.375
Noticing about how the planning
that you put into place when

00:35:12.375 --> 00:35:14.500
you first built this whole
network and the planning

00:35:14.500 --> 00:35:16.500
that you've had subsequently
since about looking

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:18.750
to the future, I'm curious
to know, though, what's

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:20.790
been the most surprising
or possibly the most

00:35:20.790 --> 00:35:23.240
whimsical thing that you've
seen happen on the internet,

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:25.140
that you were like,
that was never--

00:35:25.140 --> 00:35:27.240
I never thought that
was going to happen?

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:28.760
VINT CERF: Well, let's see.

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:30.570
Certainly to be
fair about this, I

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:34.740
think the arrival of the World
Wide Web was quite a surprise.

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:37.590
I had seen something
similar to that Douglas

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:41.100
Engelbart developed and
demonstrated as early as 1968.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:42.870
In fact, if your
listeners would like

00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:46.590
to go online and Google
the mother of all demos,

00:35:46.590 --> 00:35:48.450
you will see something
quite astonishing,

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:51.780
which is the demonstration
of a kind of World Wide

00:35:51.780 --> 00:35:55.830
Web in a box, in one machine
at SRI International in Menlo

00:35:55.830 --> 00:35:57.520
Park, California.

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:01.230
Much of the World
Wide Web is picked up

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:03.570
in that particular
demonstration.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:05.250
He had hyperlinking.

00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:08.790
For example, of documents
produced in the online system.

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:10.590
He built a mouse
so that you could

00:36:10.590 --> 00:36:12.960
point to things on the
screen and click on them,

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:16.410
to say pay attention to
that, or go to that document,

00:36:16.410 --> 00:36:19.360
or perform and edit this
document at the place where

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:20.550
I'm pointing.

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:23.130
So he had to invent the mouse.

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:25.369
He invented portrait
mode displays,

00:36:25.369 --> 00:36:27.410
which made things look
more like a sheet of paper

00:36:27.410 --> 00:36:32.520
than the sort of yellow,
and green displays

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:35.160
that were common in the day.

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:37.650
So Tim Berners-Lee
of course comes along

00:36:37.650 --> 00:36:42.450
in late 1991 at
CERN in Switzerland

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:47.190
with an expansive network-based
idea for a similar thing.

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:50.220
I'm not sure whether he even
knew about the online system

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:52.420
from Englebart at the time.

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:56.660
And of course, this evolves
into Netscape Communications

00:36:56.660 --> 00:37:00.800
product, with the follow
on to the Mosaic browser.

00:37:00.800 --> 00:37:03.510
And it suddenly
triggered this avalanche

00:37:03.510 --> 00:37:05.880
of content being
created by people

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:08.120
who were able to either
write their own HTML

00:37:08.120 --> 00:37:10.890
or had an application
that did that for them.

00:37:10.890 --> 00:37:13.890
I was totally blown away
by the amount of content

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:15.970
that suddenly
showed up on the net

00:37:15.970 --> 00:37:18.120
that people just
wanted to share.

00:37:18.120 --> 00:37:20.890
They weren't asking for any
kind of compensation at all.

00:37:20.890 --> 00:37:22.740
They just thought,
well, maybe people

00:37:22.740 --> 00:37:26.550
would find what I know
interesting to them.

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:29.640
And so I'm seeing this
pile of stuff just flowing

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:32.550
into the net in the
mid-1990s thinking--

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:34.990
and then of course,
Google comes along

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:40.710
with its search engine
following Altavista, and Archie,

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:43.710
and several other
things, Yahoo, and so on,

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:46.060
which helped to try to
make sense out of this

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:48.330
is increasing pile of content.

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:51.690
I was amazed that people would
share so much information

00:37:51.690 --> 00:37:54.300
without expectation
of compensation.

00:37:54.300 --> 00:37:55.950
And I'm pretty sure
they were doing it

00:37:55.950 --> 00:37:58.380
for the satisfaction of
knowing that what they knew

00:37:58.380 --> 00:38:00.690
might be useful
to somebody else.

00:38:00.690 --> 00:38:05.100
I think there is this
secret desire in all of us

00:38:05.100 --> 00:38:06.840
to believe that
we've done something

00:38:06.840 --> 00:38:08.250
that's useful for someone else.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:11.340
We've shared something that's
helpful to someone else.

00:38:11.340 --> 00:38:13.500
And the internet
is a perfect medium

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:14.850
in which to try to do that.

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:16.810
So that certainly surprised me.

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:20.730
Now, as far as
funny things-- well,

00:38:20.730 --> 00:38:24.030
of course, when the first
internet picture frame showed

00:38:24.030 --> 00:38:26.294
up I remember thinking, what?

00:38:26.294 --> 00:38:28.460
What do you mean the
internet-enabled picture frame?

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:28.959
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:28.959 --> 00:38:30.260
What does that mean?

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:33.150
And of course, people said,
well it downloads images

00:38:33.150 --> 00:38:35.370
from a website and then
cycles through them.

00:38:35.370 --> 00:38:37.500
And that means that
grandparents can see what

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:38.790
their grandchildren are doing.

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:40.170
When they get up in
the morning, they just

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:42.490
look at the picture frame,
and it's just updated itself

00:38:42.490 --> 00:38:44.860
from the latest website.

00:38:44.860 --> 00:38:46.750
And that surprised me.

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:49.125
On the other hand,
I do remember,

00:38:49.125 --> 00:38:55.150
I think it was probably in the
early 1990s or maybe late '80s,

00:38:55.150 --> 00:38:58.860
there was a convention
or an exposition

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:02.570
called Interop, which
stood for interoperability,

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:05.010
that was started by a
friend of mine, Dan Lynch.

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:07.740
And during one of
the Interop shows,

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:11.130
somebody decided to put a
toaster up on the network

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:14.560
so you could send an SNMP
control packet, say, how

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:15.980
burned you wanted your toast.

00:39:15.980 --> 00:39:18.210
And we were all saying,
oh, isn't that funny.

00:39:18.210 --> 00:39:19.680
Ha ha ha.

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.600
And then someday everybody will
have internet-enabled devices

00:39:22.600 --> 00:39:23.100
on them.

00:39:23.100 --> 00:39:24.420
Well, now here we are.

00:39:24.420 --> 00:39:27.960
The internet of things
is rapidly flowing on us

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:30.150
But I remember at the time
we were all thinking that

00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:31.290
would be pretty funny.

00:39:31.290 --> 00:39:34.010
I used to make jokes
about, oh, yes, some day

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:36.670
every light bulb will
have its own IP address.

00:39:36.670 --> 00:39:37.710
Well, guess what.

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:40.620
Philips makes an
internet-controled light bulb,

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:41.880
as do others, I suppose.

00:39:41.880 --> 00:39:43.980
And they all have
their IP addresses.

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:47.710
So you know, don't make fun
of things, because who knows.

00:39:47.710 --> 00:39:49.307
They may come true.

00:39:49.307 --> 00:39:50.640
So those are some of the things.

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:53.310
And I suppose the
obligatory observation--

00:39:53.310 --> 00:39:56.370
they never expected as many cat
videos to show up on the net

00:39:56.370 --> 00:39:57.030
as have.

00:39:57.030 --> 00:39:57.780
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:57.780 --> 00:39:58.650
Wow.

00:39:58.650 --> 00:40:01.140
Who spends their
time watching cats?

00:40:01.140 --> 00:40:03.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK, so you
mentioned Tim Berners-Lee

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:04.830
and the World Wide Web.

00:40:04.830 --> 00:40:08.400
And as part of the
research for this episode,

00:40:08.400 --> 00:40:10.530
I'm kind of forced at
asking this question.

00:40:10.530 --> 00:40:14.802
I've seen a picture of the two
of you wearing both T-shirts.

00:40:14.802 --> 00:40:17.790
Where you're wearing
a T-shirt that says,

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:20.340
I did not invent the web.

00:40:20.340 --> 00:40:21.780
And he's wearing
a T-shirt saying,

00:40:21.780 --> 00:40:25.620
I did not invent the
internet, which is by itself

00:40:25.620 --> 00:40:28.710
an amazing T-shirt, but I
think the important part here

00:40:28.710 --> 00:40:34.110
is that it is the
only picture I've ever

00:40:34.110 --> 00:40:37.782
seen of you not wearing a suit.

00:40:37.782 --> 00:40:38.490
VINT CERF: Right.

00:40:38.490 --> 00:40:40.865
Well, and I kind of
put that over my coat.

00:40:40.865 --> 00:40:41.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Of course.

00:40:41.990 --> 00:40:44.550
VINT CERF: So there's a story
about the three piece suits

00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:47.694
if you want to finish this
interview with that story.

00:40:47.694 --> 00:40:48.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Absolutely.

00:40:48.860 --> 00:40:49.290
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:40:49.290 --> 00:40:49.560
VINT CERF: All right.

00:40:49.560 --> 00:40:49.710
MARK MANDEL: Absolutely.

00:40:49.710 --> 00:40:51.459
VINT CERF: So this is
a very simple story.

00:40:51.459 --> 00:40:52.950
I'm at Stanford University.

00:40:52.950 --> 00:40:55.490
I've been running the internet
research program from there

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:57.150
as an assistant professor.

00:40:57.150 --> 00:40:58.957
And the Advanced
Research Projects Agency

00:40:58.957 --> 00:41:00.540
says, why don't you
come to Washington

00:41:00.540 --> 00:41:03.720
and just run the whole thing,
and run the packet satellite

00:41:03.720 --> 00:41:05.580
program, and the
packet radio program,

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:08.520
and the packet security
program along with internet.

00:41:08.520 --> 00:41:09.540
So I agree.

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.640
And my wife, who's from Kansas
but was with me at Stanford

00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:14.580
says you're going to Washington?

00:41:14.580 --> 00:41:15.810
Or we're going to Washington?

00:41:15.810 --> 00:41:16.580
Three-piece suits.

00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:19.050
So she goes out and she
buys three three-piece suits

00:41:19.050 --> 00:41:22.140
from Saks Fifth Avenue at
Stanford Shopping Center,

00:41:22.140 --> 00:41:23.925
including a seersucker
three-piece suit

00:41:23.925 --> 00:41:25.300
because she knows
that it's going

00:41:25.300 --> 00:41:28.450
to be hot and humid during
the summertime in Washington.

00:41:28.450 --> 00:41:32.580
So I show up around
August or so at ARPA.

00:41:32.580 --> 00:41:35.190
And the next thing
I know, I am asked

00:41:35.190 --> 00:41:38.900
to go testify at some
committee at the Congress.

00:41:38.900 --> 00:41:42.690
And I'm wearing my
three-piece seersucker suit.

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:44.840
So I show up, and
I do my testimony.

00:41:44.840 --> 00:41:47.330
And I go back to
ARPA, and weeks go by.

00:41:47.330 --> 00:41:50.160
And one day, the director of
ARPA calls me into his office

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:52.380
and says, I want to
talk to you about

00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:54.530
your Congressional testimony.

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:56.910
And I'm thinking,
did I screw up?

00:41:56.910 --> 00:41:58.660
Am I about to lose
my government job?

00:41:58.660 --> 00:42:00.450
Is it the end of the world?

00:42:00.450 --> 00:42:02.110
And so I sit down,
and he says, oh, I

00:42:02.110 --> 00:42:04.176
have a letter from the
chairman of the committee

00:42:04.176 --> 00:42:04.800
in front of me.

00:42:04.800 --> 00:42:07.530
He says thank you very much
for Dr. Cerf's testimony.

00:42:07.530 --> 00:42:09.840
By the way, he's the
best-dressed guy from ARPA

00:42:09.840 --> 00:42:11.531
we've ever seen.

00:42:11.531 --> 00:42:12.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:12.030 --> 00:42:14.220
And I took that as
positive feedback.

00:42:14.220 --> 00:42:16.440
So I've been wearing
three-piece suits ever since.

00:42:16.440 --> 00:42:17.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is amazing.

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:18.150
That has not changed yet.

00:42:18.150 --> 00:42:21.791
I think you are still probably
the best-dressed person in tech

00:42:21.791 --> 00:42:22.290
that I know.

00:42:22.290 --> 00:42:25.020
VINT CERF: Well, I ought to
tilt my camera here so that you

00:42:25.020 --> 00:42:27.870
can see that I am indeed
wearing a three-piece suit here

00:42:27.870 --> 00:42:29.010
in my Washington office.

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:31.987
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm so happy
I am recording this right now.

00:42:31.987 --> 00:42:34.320
MARK MANDEL: For those who
are listening on the podcast,

00:42:34.320 --> 00:42:37.320
Vint is wearing a
three-piece suit right now.

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:39.510
VINT CERF: Well,
actually it was very

00:42:39.510 --> 00:42:41.660
appropriate to wear a
three-piece suit in any case,

00:42:41.660 --> 00:42:44.520
as I was at the Inter-American
Development Bank

00:42:44.520 --> 00:42:47.110
with a whole lot of
very senior people.

00:42:47.110 --> 00:42:50.680
And so I think it's a sign of
respect to show up well-dressed

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.670
when you were speaking to people
who are more important than you

00:42:53.670 --> 00:42:54.204
are.

00:42:54.204 --> 00:42:56.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm so
sad right now that I'm

00:42:56.120 --> 00:42:58.890
weren't just a T-shirt.

00:42:58.890 --> 00:43:01.010
MARK MANDEL: Well,
Vint, unfortunately we

00:43:01.010 --> 00:43:02.010
are running out of time.

00:43:02.010 --> 00:43:03.750
I would love to spend more
time chatting with you,

00:43:03.750 --> 00:43:05.970
but I really appreciate
you taking the time out

00:43:05.970 --> 00:43:07.910
of your day to chat with
us, and come with us

00:43:07.910 --> 00:43:09.557
and celebrate our 100th episode.

00:43:09.557 --> 00:43:11.640
It's been absolutely lovely
and a complete delight

00:43:11.640 --> 00:43:12.717
speaking to you today.

00:43:12.717 --> 00:43:14.550
VINT CERF: Well, thank
you so much for that.

00:43:14.550 --> 00:43:18.690
I am looking forward to spending
more time with the Google Cloud

00:43:18.690 --> 00:43:19.900
team.

00:43:19.900 --> 00:43:23.130
And as you know, we are making
some very serious investments

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:25.410
not only in increased
scale, but also

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:27.720
the diversity in the system.

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:29.940
So as time goes on,
we are going to see

00:43:29.940 --> 00:43:33.810
not only our conventional CPUs
but the graphics processing

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:39.660
units the GPUs, and now
increasingly the TensorFlow

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:43.350
processing units for doing
multilevel neural networks.

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:47.160
And eventually, assuming
we're successful,

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:49.320
we will have quantum
processing units

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:51.450
that will allow
people to do things

00:43:51.450 --> 00:43:54.060
faster than they ever could
before for certain kinds

00:43:54.060 --> 00:43:54.960
of optimization.

00:43:54.960 --> 00:43:59.700
So the system which started
out being a warehouse computing

00:43:59.700 --> 00:44:03.150
commodity commonality
is now going

00:44:03.150 --> 00:44:05.880
to become a much more diverse
computing platform that

00:44:05.880 --> 00:44:09.150
can specialize in to
solve particular problems

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:10.620
that our customers have.

00:44:10.620 --> 00:44:14.760
So I'm very excited about this
new heterogeneous computing

00:44:14.760 --> 00:44:18.090
environment that Google
brings to its customer base

00:44:18.090 --> 00:44:19.700
through the Google Cloud.

00:44:19.700 --> 00:44:22.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is
definitely very exciting.

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:24.900
And if you want to come
back to the podcast

00:44:24.900 --> 00:44:27.390
and tell us more about it,
we're definitely, definitely

00:44:27.390 --> 00:44:28.320
down for that.

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:29.950
VINT CERF: I'd be
delighted to do that.

00:44:29.950 --> 00:44:30.864
MARK MANDEL: Anytime.

00:44:30.864 --> 00:44:32.280
All right, well
thank you so much.

00:44:32.280 --> 00:44:32.820
VINT CERF: OK.

00:44:32.820 --> 00:44:33.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Thank you very much.

00:44:33.120 --> 00:44:33.619
Goodbye.

00:44:33.619 --> 00:44:35.220
VINT CERF: See you
guys on the net.

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:37.015
MARK MANDEL: Oh my God, we
